Flue-Gouge
Ingredients: K-R&B + NU JAZZ / ELECTRO JAZZ + REBEL, ANGRY & AGGRESSIVE + Taiko  
Creative Catalyst: Invented by miners two kilometers underground, drumming on ventilation pipes.

Flue-Gouge is a suffocating, hyper-syncopated sonic rebellion where the silky, honeyed melisma of modern K-R&B is dragged through coal dust, snarled through half-choked respirators, and collided with jagged electro-jazz harmonies. Beneath this tense vocal beauty sits an apocalyptic rhythm section: colossal, skin-splitting Taiko strikes delivered with iron mallets directly against hollow, resonant subterranean ventilation flues.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 76–88 BPM half-time swing, counterbalanced by blistering 32nd-note Taiko polyrhythms and broken-beat electro-jazz drum flourishes that give the illusion of frantic kinetic velocity within a slow, heavy swagger.
- Harmonic Palette: Dissonant minor 9th and diminished 11th chord clusters played on distorted electric pianos, punctuated by microtonal pansori vocal bends and whole-tone brass stabs that mimic industrial pressure valves.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Borehole Flue-Feeding: Lead vocals and muted brass are piped through a 2,000-meter pressurized galvanized copper tube to create metallic, fluttery resonance without digital reverb.
  2. Blast-Seismic Gating: Raw Taiko low-end frequencies are sidechained directly to seismic blast sensors, slamming the rest of the mix into total, suffocating vacuum silence on every downbeat.
  3. Barometric Breath Compression: Vocal tracks are aggressively dynamic-compressed against the performer's actual mechanical respirator valves, making every gasp for air drive the synth-bass distortion.
- Dynamics: Abrupt, violent drops from whispering, intimate K-R&B crooning over a single sparse Rhodes progression directly into concussive, full-frequency percussive assaults that rattle the listener's ribcage.

Scene & Origin Lore
Flue-Gouge was born during the four-week wildcat strike in Shaft 9 of the Taebaek Deep Ore Project in Gangwon Province. Cut off from fresh air and surface communications when management shut down the automated intake turbines, two dozen miners refused to die quietly. Striking engineer-musicians ripped the mufflers off the ventilation trunks, transforming two kilometers of hollow metal flues into massive acoustic horns and percussive resonators beaten with steel wrenches. The subculture that emerged from that survival siege wears flame-retardant flight suits customized with frayed black silk linings, face-shield respirators etched with protest slogans, and weighted steel-toed combat boots wired with acoustic kick-triggers. Their illicit underground live shows—held in abandoned drainage vaults—begin with twenty seconds of pitch-black silence, broken only by the synchronized clicking of respirator valves before the first Taiko blow shatters the dark.

Typical Instruments
- Percussion: Galvanized ventilation ducting (flues) beaten with sledgehammers, Nagado-daiko wrapped in damp canvas, broken-beat electronic drum pads.
- Keys & Synths: Overdriven Fender Rhodes Mark I, Moog Sub 37 routed through ring modulators, seismic borehole sensor transducers.
- Horns: Muted pocket trumpet, bass trombone played with flutter-tongue techniques into contact microphones.
- Vocals: Multi-tracked, agile K-R&B falsettos interspersed with guttural, hoarse union-chant shouts.

